Task 4 — DNS (about 97 words)
DNS (Domain Name System) is the internet's phonebook. People remember names like 
epicreads.com, but computers connect using IP addresses.
DNS translates the domain name into the server's IP address so the browser knows 
where to send the request. My friend should create an A record, 
because an A record maps a domain name to an IPv4 address like 52.172.142.222.
(AAAA is for IPv6, and CNAME points to another domain.) 
DNS does not store ports, so port 3000 must be handled by a reverse proxy 
such as Nginx forwarding port 80/443 to 3000, or by adding :3000 to the URL.
